Speaker: Prof. L. Hartwell, School of Information

Abstract: In 1962 Douglas Engelbart framed computing as a way to augment huma=
n intellect, not to automate it. Most of what we build today, including AI as=
sistants, optimizes for the automation half. This talk revisits the original =
"augmentation" framework (artifacts, language, methodology, training), shows =
three recent systems that take it seriously, and argues that provenance, know=
ing where a thought came from, is the missing piece in current tools for thou=
ght.

